erating in real-world environments. This interdisciplinary approach is exemplified by MIT\u2019s efforts in the field of neuro-symbolic AI, which seeks to combine the strengths of symbolic reasoning (the ability to manipulate abstract concepts) with the learning capabilities of neural networks. Researchers at MIT, such as Josh Tenenbaum and his colleagues, have been at the forefront of this movement, developing models that can reason about the world in a more human-like manner. These efforts highlight MIT\u2019s unique ability to bridge the gap between different fields of study, leading to AI systems that are not only powerful but also more interpretable and aligned with human values.\n\nMIT\u2019s influence on AI is also evident in its role as an incubator for startups and entrepreneurs who have gone on to shape the AI industry. The institute\u2019s proximity to the vibrant startup ecosystem of Boston and its strong ties to Silicon Valley have made it a breeding ground for AI-driven innovation. Many of the companies that have revolutionized AI, such as iRobot, which developed the Roomba vacuum cleaner, and Kiva Systems (now part of Amazon Robotics), which pioneered warehouse automation, trace their roots back to MIT. The institute\u2019s entrepreneurship programs, such as the Martin Trust Center for MIT Entrepreneurship, provide students and researchers with the resources and support they need to turn their ideas into successful businesses. This culture of entrepreneurship has led to the creation of numerous AI startups that have introduced groundbreaking technologies to the market. For example, companies like DataRobot, founded by MIT alumni, have democratized machine learning by providing platforms that allow businesses to build and deploy AI models without requiring deep technical expertise. Similarly, startups like DeepMind, which was co-founded by Demis Hassabis, a former MIT researcher, have pushed the boundaries of AI by developing systems capable of masteri